1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,200
[A green, perfectly round young hill rising out of a suburb, houses all around its foot. Kites over the top.]

2
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:05,215
NARRATOR: Auckland has fifty-four volcanoes. I watched the newest one being born.

3
00:00:05,485 --> 00:00:08,635
[Title card.]

4
00:00:05,585 --> 00:00:08,575
NARRATOR: It took four days. This is how it happened.

5
00:00:09,345 --> 00:00:12,495
[Monday breakfast in a wooden villa: Dad in his bus-driver shirt; Mum in a red fleece with her ID lanyard, heading out; Lui at the back door with a tin of fish food for the pond; Ana with her notebook.]

6
00:00:09,445 --> 00:00:13,385
NARRATOR: Lui, Goldie and Spot get a pinch each, not the whole tin.

7
00:00:13,855 --> 00:00:17,005
[The door closes behind Mum. Dad grabs his keys.]

8
00:00:13,955 --> 00:00:18,545
NARRATOR: Mum studied volcanoes. Dad drove the number 72 bus.

9
00:00:18,815 --> 00:00:21,965
[Reverse: narrator answers.]

10
00:00:18,915 --> 00:00:22,880
NARRATOR: Lui had two goldfish in the pond. And I had a notebook.

11
00:00:23,150 --> 00:00:25,000
[A bush creek behind a school: ferns, a footbridge. A class in raincoats taking readings. Ana kneels at the water with a thermometer and a notebook.]

12
00:00:23,250 --> 00:00:24,740
NARRATOR: I was twelve.

13
00:00:25,150 --> 00:00:28,300
[Reverse: narrator answers.]

14
00:00:25,250 --> 00:00:29,115
NARRATOR: Every Monday, my class tested the creek behind our school.

15
00:00:29,385 --> 00:00:32,140
[Close on Ana reading the thermometer.]

16
00:00:29,485 --> 00:00:32,020
NARRATOR: Fourteen degrees. Same as always.

17
00:00:32,290 --> 00:00:35,050
[Her classmate Jaz peers at the notebook, grinning.]

18
00:00:32,390 --> 00:00:34,930
NARRATOR: Fourteen again? You and your nerd notebook.

19
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:37,555
[Reverse: ana answers.]

20
00:00:35,300 --> 00:00:37,135
NARRATOR: Nerds notice things.

21
00:00:37,705 --> 00:00:40,615
[A sharp jolt. The water ripples in rings, the trees shiver, a bird flock bursts up. The teacher steps forward.]

22
00:00:37,805 --> 00:00:40,495
NARRATOR: Everyone, step away from the trees!

23
00:00:40,765 --> 00:00:43,915
[A phone emergency alert: VOLCANIC UNREST · AUCKLAND · Earthquakes under the city. Stay informed.]

24
00:00:40,865 --> 00:00:44,805
NARRATOR: By lunchtime, every phone in the city showed the same alert.

25
00:00:45,075 --> 00:00:48,225
[Cut-away under the city: a thin finger of glowing magma deep below, a cloud of tiny flashes around its tip. Depth readout: 10 KM.]

26
00:00:45,175 --> 00:00:50,940
NARRATOR: Ten kilometres under the city, hot melted rock was rising, cracking its way up.

27
00:00:51,210 --> 00:00:54,360
[A map of Auckland: one by one, about fifty volcano dots light up across the city, harbour to harbour.]

28
00:00:51,310 --> 00:00:54,425
NARRATOR: Auckland is built on about fifty volcanoes.

29
00:00:54,695 --> 00:00:57,845
[Continues.]

30
00:00:54,795 --> 00:00:58,860
NARRATOR: Almost all of them erupted just once, each in a new place.

31
00:00:59,130 --> 00:01:02,280
[The same map: a big pulsing question mark drifts over the city.]

32
00:00:59,230 --> 00:01:02,520
NARRATOR: So nobody could say where the next one would come up.

33
00:01:02,790 --> 00:01:05,300
[The Tupou family villa at dinner: Dad in his bus-driver shirt, Lui, Ana. An empty chair. The TV news shows a map with dots.]

34
00:01:02,890 --> 00:01:05,180
NARRATOR: Mum didn't come home for dinner.

35
00:01:05,450 --> 00:01:08,435
[Reverse: narrator answers.]

36
00:01:05,550 --> 00:01:08,315
NARRATOR: She was at the monitoring centre all night.

37
00:01:08,585 --> 00:01:11,735
[Ana holds up the phone: Mum on a video call from the monitoring centre.]

38
00:01:08,685 --> 00:01:13,675
NARRATOR: It's about ten kilometres down, and it's rising. It could take a few days.

39
00:01:14,145 --> 00:01:16,175
[Reverse: ana answers.]

40
00:01:14,245 --> 00:01:15,855
NARRATOR: Where will it come out?

41
00:01:16,325 --> 00:01:19,475
[At the monitoring centre, a wall-sized map behind her, Mum on the phone.]

42
00:01:16,425 --> 00:01:19,640
NARRATOR: We don't know yet. That's what we're trying to work out.

43
00:01:19,910 --> 00:01:21,945
[The cut-away again: traffic and trains on the surface shake the picture; the flashes around the magma blur into a wide fuzzy circle.]

44
00:01:20,010 --> 00:01:21,825
NARRATOR: A city is noisy.

45
00:01:22,095 --> 00:01:25,245
[Continues.]

46
00:01:22,195 --> 00:01:28,560
NARRATOR: Trucks and trains blur tiny earthquakes, so the scientists could only roughly tell where they were.

47
00:01:28,830 --> 00:01:31,935
[After dinner. Lui, in pyjamas, grips the back of the sofa as a small jolt rattles the cups.]

48
00:01:28,930 --> 00:01:31,815
NARRATOR: Is the volcano going to come up under my bed?

49
00:01:32,085 --> 00:01:35,235
[Ana crouches to his height.]

50
00:01:32,185 --> 00:01:35,270
NARRATOR: No. The city is huge, and your bed is tiny.

51
00:01:35,540 --> 00:01:38,690
[A stronger jolt. The family drops under the dining table, Dad’s arm over the kids.]

52
00:01:35,640 --> 00:01:39,105
NARRATOR: Drop, cover, hold! Under the table, both of you!

53
00:01:39,375 --> 00:01:42,525
[Dad puts a packed backpack by the front door: water, a torch, a radio, a first-aid kit.]

54
00:01:39,475 --> 00:01:45,090
NARRATOR: Just in case. Water, torch, radio, and the first-aid kit. It lives by the door now.

55
00:01:45,360 --> 00:01:47,715
[The next morning, before school. Ana alone at the creek. The water is fizzing with tiny bubbles all along the bank.]

56
00:01:45,460 --> 00:01:47,595
NARRATOR: It's fizzing. Like lemonade.

57
00:01:47,865 --> 00:01:51,015
[Ana’s notebook: a column of Monday readings, 14 °C, 14 °C, 14 °C… and today, circled: 19 °C. Bubbles!]

58
00:01:47,965 --> 00:01:52,155
NARRATOR: Nineteen degrees. Overnight, the creek had warmed up by five.

59
00:01:52,425 --> 00:01:55,575
[A classroom. The teacher at a whiteboard covered in volcano dots. Ana at her desk with her notebook open.]

60
00:01:52,525 --> 00:01:58,990
NARRATOR: Nearly every volcano in Auckland is a one-off. It erupts once, and then never again.

61
00:01:59,260 --> 00:02:02,410
[The class goes quiet, looking at the dots.]

62
00:01:59,360 --> 00:02:05,025
NARRATOR: So the next one would open somewhere brand new: under a road, a field, or a house.

63
00:02:05,295 --> 00:02:08,445
[At lunch, on a phone: a press conference. Mum at the microphones, a map behind her with a red circle out under the harbour.]

64
00:02:05,395 --> 00:02:10,185
NEWS ANCHOR: Scientists say their best guess is under the harbour, but they can't be sure yet.

65
00:02:10,455 --> 00:02:13,035
[The back garden after school. Lui kneels by the little pond at the bottom of the sloping lawn. Two goldfish float on the surface.]

66
00:02:10,555 --> 00:02:12,915
NARRATOR: Ana! Goldie and Spot aren't moving!

67
00:02:13,185 --> 00:02:16,335
[Ana kneels beside him and looks up the slope: the pond sits in the lowest dip of the garden.]

68
00:02:13,285 --> 00:02:17,668
NARRATOR: They were fine this morning. And the pond's at the very bottom of the garden.

69
00:02:17,938 --> 00:02:21,088
[A suburban street lined with wooden villas; a park with big trees at the far end. Dad’s city bus rolls over a smooth new hump in the road; the back of the bus scrapes with a shower of sparks.]

70
00:02:18,038 --> 00:02:22,553
NARRATOR: On his last run, the back of Dad’s bus scraped on a bump in the road.

71
00:02:22,823 --> 00:02:25,283
[Reverse: narrator answers.]

72
00:02:22,923 --> 00:02:25,163
NARRATOR: It hadn’t been there last week.

73
00:02:25,433 --> 00:02:28,583
[Dinner. Dad, puzzled.]

74
00:02:25,533 --> 00:02:29,798
NARRATOR: There's a new bump on Kōwhai Street. My bus scraped right over it.

75
00:02:30,068 --> 00:02:33,048
[Ana stops eating. Fork in the air.]

76
00:02:30,168 --> 00:02:32,928
NARRATOR: Kōwhai Street? That's right next to the creek.

77
00:02:33,198 --> 00:02:35,408
[Ana’s notebook map of the neighbourhood in coloured pen: a star at the creek, a fish at the pond, a bus at the bump. She draws a circle around all three. Their own house is in the middle.]

78
00:02:33,298 --> 00:02:35,288
NARRATOR: I put all three on a map.

79
00:02:35,558 --> 00:02:38,618
[Continues.]

80
00:02:35,658 --> 00:02:38,498
NARRATOR: They were all within a kilometre of our house.

81
00:02:38,768 --> 00:02:41,918
[Diagram: gas bubbling up through the ground ahead of the magma, fizzing out of the creek and pooling, invisible but tinted, in the low pond.]

82
00:02:38,868 --> 00:02:43,033
NARRATOR: Before magma reaches the surface, gas leaks out ahead of it.

83
00:02:43,303 --> 00:02:46,453
[Continues.]

84
00:02:43,403 --> 00:02:48,043
NARRATOR: That gas is heavier than air, so it sinks into hollows and ponds.

85
00:02:48,313 --> 00:02:51,463
[Diagram: the magma swelling up from below; the road surface bulging above it; a GPS station’s arrow climbing.]

86
00:02:48,413 --> 00:02:52,528
NARRATOR: And as the magma pushes upward, the ground above it rises.

87
00:02:52,798 --> 00:02:55,948
[Ana stands up from the table, notebook in hand.]

88
00:02:52,898 --> 00:02:56,433
NARRATOR: Dad, can you drive me to the creek? I want to check it again.

89
00:02:56,703 --> 00:02:59,438
[Reverse: sione answers.]

90
00:02:56,803 --> 00:02:59,018
NARRATOR: Get your coat. I'll get the torch.

91
00:02:59,588 --> 00:03:02,718
[Night at the creek. Dad’s torch on the fizzing water. Ana reads the thermometer.]

92
00:02:59,688 --> 00:03:02,598
NARRATOR: Twenty-one degrees. It's still getting warmer.

93
00:03:02,868 --> 00:03:06,018
[Kōwhai Street, the hump in the torchlight. A thin crack runs across the hump; Ana holds her palm over it.]

94
00:03:02,968 --> 00:03:06,328
NARRATOR: Dad, feel this. Warm air is coming out of the crack.

95
00:03:06,598 --> 00:03:08,833
[Reverse: sione answers.]

96
00:03:06,698 --> 00:03:08,413
NARRATOR: Call your mother. Now.

97
00:03:08,983 --> 00:03:12,038
[By the dark front window, Ana calls Mum.]

98
00:03:09,083 --> 00:03:11,918
NARRATOR: Mum, I think I know where it’s going to come up.

99
00:03:12,188 --> 00:03:15,338
[Mum at the centre, exhausted, screens everywhere.]

100
00:03:12,288 --> 00:03:16,153
NARRATOR: Ana, love, I'm really busy. Can it wait until tomorrow?

101
00:03:16,423 --> 00:03:19,573
[Ana, steady.]

102
00:03:16,523 --> 00:03:20,008
NARRATOR: No. Our creek is fizzing, and it’s five degrees warmer.

103
00:03:20,278 --> 00:03:23,428
[Closer.]

104
00:03:20,378 --> 00:03:24,838
NARRATOR: Lui's fish died in the hollow, and the road on Kōwhai Street is rising.

105
00:03:25,108 --> 00:03:28,193
[Mum stops. Turns slowly toward the map.]

106
00:03:25,208 --> 00:03:27,473
NARRATOR: Say that again. Slowly.

107
00:03:28,343 --> 00:03:31,493
[A colleague points at a screen: the graph of the nearest GPS station climbing steeply since the morning.]

108
00:03:28,443 --> 00:03:32,853
NARRATOR: The nearest GPS station has risen four centimetres since this morning.

109
00:03:33,123 --> 00:03:36,273
[Reverse: mele answers.]

110
00:03:33,223 --> 00:03:38,213
NARRATOR: We have a handful of instruments for a whole city. She just gave us three more.

111
00:03:38,783 --> 00:03:41,933
[Mum, already moving.]

112
00:03:38,883 --> 00:03:42,548
NARRATOR: Send the gas team to Kōwhai Street. Right now.

113
00:03:42,818 --> 00:03:45,968
[Midnight. Two scientists in high-vis on the hump in the road, a gas meter flashing red and beeping.]

114
00:03:42,918 --> 00:03:48,058
NARRATOR: Just before midnight, the gas team's meters started beeping on Kōwhai Street.

115
00:03:48,328 --> 00:03:51,478
[One in the morning. Ana asleep in the armchair by the window, the phone buzzing in her hand.]

116
00:03:48,428 --> 00:03:52,618
NARRATOR: Ana? The gas team found it. You were right. Pack a bag, love.

117
00:03:53,288 --> 00:03:56,438
[The map: a big red evacuation circle centred out under the harbour slides inland and shrinks onto one suburb. Ana’s street is near its centre.]

118
00:03:53,388 --> 00:03:56,953
NARRATOR: On Wednesday morning, the evacuation zone moved.

119
00:03:57,223 --> 00:03:59,583
[Continues.]

120
00:03:57,323 --> 00:03:59,463
NARRATOR: Our street was right in the middle.

121
00:03:59,733 --> 00:04:02,693
[The phone alert: EVACUATE NOW · Zone A · Leave within 2 hours. Take only what you need.]

122
00:03:59,833 --> 00:04:02,573
NARRATOR: Every phone in the zone told us to leave.

123
00:04:02,843 --> 00:04:05,993
[The lounge in a whirl: bags open on the sofa. Dad in his uniform, keys in hand.]

124
00:04:02,943 --> 00:04:06,183
NARRATOR: Twenty minutes. Take the things you can’t replace.

125
00:04:06,453 --> 00:04:09,603
[Ana takes a framed family photo off the wall and her notebook off the table.]

126
00:04:06,553 --> 00:04:09,843
NARRATOR: I took a photo of the four of us, and my notebook.

127
00:04:10,113 --> 00:04:13,263
[Next door, old Mrs. Kaur is lifting the hatch to her cellar under the house: her cat has run down there. Ana sprints over.]

128
00:04:10,213 --> 00:04:14,273
NARRATOR: Mrs. Kaur, don't go down there! The gas sinks into low places.

129
00:04:14,543 --> 00:04:17,693
[Ana props the hatch open, shakes a box of cat biscuits, and the cat strolls out into its carrier.]

130
00:04:14,643 --> 00:04:17,983
NARRATOR: I shook a box of cat biscuits at the hatch instead.

131
00:04:18,253 --> 00:04:20,863
[Reverse: narrator answers.]

132
00:04:18,353 --> 00:04:20,743
NARRATOR: Cats always come for biscuits.

133
00:04:21,013 --> 00:04:23,798
[Dad lowers the bus ramp and wheels Mr. Tane up it in his wheelchair.]

134
00:04:21,113 --> 00:04:23,678
NARRATOR: Dad lowered the ramp for Mr. Tane.

135
00:04:23,948 --> 00:04:26,908
[Reverse: narrator answers.]

136
00:04:24,048 --> 00:04:26,788
NARRATOR: Nobody on our street was left behind.

137
00:04:27,058 --> 00:04:30,208
[Dad’s bus at the kerb, doors open. Neighbours queue with bags; old Mrs. Kaur with a cat in a carrier. Ana and Lui behind her.]

138
00:04:27,158 --> 00:04:30,648
NARRATOR: On you get, Mrs. Kaur. Your cat rides free today.

139
00:04:30,918 --> 00:04:34,068
[Ana stops at the bus door and looks back down the street at their villa and the park beyond it.]

140
00:04:31,018 --> 00:04:33,783
NARRATOR: I looked back at our house from the bus door.

141
00:04:34,653 --> 00:04:37,803
[From above: every lane of the motorway flowing one way, out of the zone, buses in a long line, police at the on-ramps.]

142
00:04:34,753 --> 00:04:38,093
NARRATOR: Every lane of the motorway ran one way: out.

143
00:04:38,363 --> 00:04:41,513
[A school hall eight kilometres away, outside the zone: rows of camp beds, families, a tea urn, volunteers.]

144
00:04:38,463 --> 00:04:42,178
NARRATOR: Forty thousand people left their homes in a day and a half.

145
00:04:42,448 --> 00:04:45,598
[On the hall’s TV: Mum at the press conference, the new zone behind her.]

146
00:04:42,548 --> 00:04:47,413
NEWS ANCHOR: Scientists say a tip from a member of the public helped them find the right place.

147
00:04:47,683 --> 00:04:50,363
[Lui elbows Ana.]

148
00:04:47,783 --> 00:04:50,243
NARRATOR: That's you! You're a member of the public!

149
00:04:50,513 --> 00:04:52,518
[Then Lui goes quiet.]

150
00:04:50,613 --> 00:04:52,398
NARRATOR: Will our house be okay?

151
00:04:52,668 --> 00:04:55,621
[Reverse: ana answers.]

152
00:04:52,768 --> 00:04:55,101
NARRATOR: I don't know, Lui. But we will be.

153
00:04:55,771 --> 00:04:58,921
[Dad crouches by them for a moment, keys in hand.]

154
00:04:55,871 --> 00:04:59,511
NARRATOR: I'm going back for another load. Look after your brother.

155
00:04:59,781 --> 00:05:02,931
[Late. Lui asleep. Ana on the phone, quiet so as not to wake him.]

156
00:04:59,881 --> 00:05:04,316
NARRATOR: Mum, what if I'm wrong? Forty thousand people left because of my map.

157
00:05:04,586 --> 00:05:07,736
[Mum, at her desk, softly.]

158
00:05:04,686 --> 00:05:09,601
NARRATOR: Then they go home on Friday, a bit grumpy. Being wrong that way is fine.

159
00:05:09,871 --> 00:05:13,021
[A police drone over the empty zone: at the end of an empty street, the duck pond in the park is steaming, and the grass around it has cracked.]

160
00:05:09,971 --> 00:05:14,761
NARRATOR: On Thursday afternoon, a police drone flew over our empty street.

161
00:05:15,031 --> 00:05:17,741
[Continues.]

162
00:05:15,131 --> 00:05:17,621
NARRATOR: The duck pond in the park was steaming.

163
00:05:17,891 --> 00:05:21,041
[Mum on the phone at the centre, the earthquake screens almost solid.]

164
00:05:17,991 --> 00:05:22,506
NARRATOR: It's less than a kilometre down now. Keep everyone out of the zone.

165
00:05:22,776 --> 00:05:25,926
[Ana’s notebook, a new page: the depth of the magma, copied from the TV every hour. 5 km, 3 km, 2 km, 1.2 km, 0.8 km.]

166
00:05:22,876 --> 00:05:26,741
NARRATOR: At the hall, I wrote down the depth from the TV every hour.

167
00:05:27,011 --> 00:05:29,646
[Continues.]

168
00:05:27,111 --> 00:05:29,526
NARRATOR: The numbers kept getting smaller.

169
00:05:29,796 --> 00:05:32,946
[The hall TV: a depth gauge, less than one kilometre. ZONE A EVACUATED.]

170
00:05:29,896 --> 00:05:34,961
NEWS ANCHOR: The zone is empty, and the power there is off. Do not go back for any reason.

171
00:05:35,231 --> 00:05:38,381
[Thursday evening. Mum walks in, still in her lanyard, and hugs Ana hard.]

172
00:05:35,331 --> 00:05:38,821
NARRATOR: Everyone in the zone is out. Your map did that, Ana.

173
00:05:39,491 --> 00:05:42,641
[Ana looks at her brother.]

174
00:05:39,591 --> 00:05:42,601
NARRATOR: Lui found the fish. And Dad found the bump.

175
00:05:42,871 --> 00:05:46,021
[Mum sits with them on the bench.]

176
00:05:42,971 --> 00:05:47,336
NARRATOR: Six hundred years ago, people here watched Rangitoto erupt.

177
00:05:47,606 --> 00:05:50,756
[Grey hardened ash on an island shore: a trail of footprints, big and small, and the paw prints of a dog beside them, heading away from the sea.]

178
00:05:47,706 --> 00:05:50,996
NARRATOR: Their footprints are still there, in the hardened ash.

179
00:05:51,266 --> 00:05:54,416
[Continues.]

180
00:05:51,366 --> 00:05:55,381
NARRATOR: Grown-ups, children, and dogs. They didn't run. They walked away.

181
00:05:55,651 --> 00:05:58,611
[Time-lapse: the sea bubbles, an island rises, lava fountains build a wide gentle cone; centuries roll; it turns green with forest; ferries and picnickers.]

182
00:05:55,751 --> 00:05:58,491
NARRATOR: Rangitoto rose straight out of the sea.

183
00:05:58,761 --> 00:06:01,911
[Continues.]

184
00:05:58,861 --> 00:06:03,501
NARRATOR: Today it is covered in forest, and families walk up it on Sundays.

185
00:06:03,771 --> 00:06:06,821
[Late night in the hall, lights low. Lui asleep. Mum dozing. Ana awake, watching the high windows. The clock says 11:51.]

186
00:06:03,871 --> 00:06:05,836
NARRATOR: That night, I couldn’t sleep.

187
00:06:06,971 --> 00:06:10,121
[Across the dark city: a flash, then a white column of steam and ash punches up from the suburbs, lightning crackling in it.]

188
00:06:07,071 --> 00:06:09,986
NARRATOR: At eight minutes to midnight, the ground opened.

189
00:06:10,656 --> 00:06:13,806
[Ana at the high window, the orange glow outside. Her lips move as she counts.]

190
00:06:10,756 --> 00:06:14,621
NARRATOR: I counted the seconds, the way Dad taught us to with lightning.

191
00:06:14,891 --> 00:06:17,376
[Reverse: narrator answers.]

192
00:06:14,991 --> 00:06:16,656
NARRATOR: One, two, three…

193
00:06:17,526 --> 00:06:20,536
[Twenty-three seconds later the windows rattle in their frames with a deep boom. Everyone in the hall wakes.]

194
00:06:17,626 --> 00:06:20,416
NARRATOR: At twenty-three, the boom hit the windows.

195
00:06:20,686 --> 00:06:23,836
[Reverse: narrator answers.]

196
00:06:20,786 --> 00:06:24,651
NARRATOR: Sound takes twenty-three seconds to travel eight kilometres.

197
00:06:24,921 --> 00:06:28,071
[A volunteer with a clipboard calls names across the hall; hands go up everywhere.]

198
00:06:25,021 --> 00:06:28,461
NARRATOR: A volunteer read out every name from our street.

199
00:06:28,731 --> 00:06:31,391
[Reverse: narrator answers.]

200
00:06:28,831 --> 00:06:31,271
NARRATOR: Every single person answered.

201
00:06:31,541 --> 00:06:34,691
[Diagram: magma meets groundwater, a steam explosion, a ring of ash rushing out along the ground. The evacuation line sits beyond it.]

202
00:06:31,641 --> 00:06:39,056
NARRATOR: Where magma meets groundwater, it explodes into steam, and a cloud of ash and rock rushes out across the ground.

203
00:06:39,326 --> 00:06:41,911
[The surge sweeps empty streets; inside the zone, no one.]

204
00:06:39,426 --> 00:06:41,791
NARRATOR: Everyone it reached had already left.

205
00:06:42,061 --> 00:06:44,466
[The family at the high windows, silhouetted against an orange glow on the horizon. Dad is back. Lui tugs Mum’s sleeve.]

206
00:06:42,161 --> 00:06:44,346
NARRATOR: Is that the park near our house?

207
00:06:44,616 --> 00:06:47,766
[Mum turns to them. Dad looks down at Ana.]

208
00:06:44,716 --> 00:06:47,556
NARRATOR: Yes. It's right at the end of our street.

209
00:06:48,626 --> 00:06:51,776
[Morning: grey ash falling like snow on cars and roofs across the city; people in masks; planes parked at a closed airport.]

210
00:06:48,726 --> 00:06:53,041
NARRATOR: By morning, ash covered half the city, and the airport was closed.

211
00:06:53,311 --> 00:06:56,461
[The news over aerial pictures of a smoking crater where a park and a street used to be.]

212
00:06:53,411 --> 00:06:58,351
NEWS ANCHOR: About two hundred homes were destroyed. Nobody inside the zone was hurt.

213
00:06:58,621 --> 00:07:01,456
[Ana on the bench holding the family photo she saved. Dad beside her.]

214
00:06:58,721 --> 00:07:00,536
NARRATOR: Ours was one of them.

215
00:07:01,606 --> 00:07:04,756
[Dad nods at the hall: Mrs. Kaur with her cat, the neighbours, the kids.]

216
00:07:01,706 --> 00:07:05,446
NARRATOR: We lost a house. Look around you. We didn’t lose anyone.

217
00:07:05,716 --> 00:07:08,866
[Time-lapse: night after night a fountain of lava builds a black cone where the park was; a slow tongue of lava creeps down one street; then it goes quiet.]

218
00:07:05,816 --> 00:07:10,431
NARRATOR: For two months, it fountained lava every night and built itself a hill.

219
00:07:10,701 --> 00:07:12,711
[Continues.]

220
00:07:10,801 --> 00:07:12,591
NARRATOR: Then it stopped, for good.

221
00:07:12,861 --> 00:07:15,921
[Ana and Mum in hard hats at a safety fence. Beyond it, where Kōwhai Street ran, a grey slope of ash and a steaming crater rim. Half a red letterbox sticks out of the ash.]

222
00:07:12,961 --> 00:07:15,801
NARRATOR: When it was safe, Mum took me to the fence.

223
00:07:16,071 --> 00:07:19,221
[Continues.]

224
00:07:16,171 --> 00:07:21,511
NARRATOR: Our street was under three metres of ash. I could see the top of our letterbox.

225
00:07:21,781 --> 00:07:24,931
[A different house, half-unpacked. Ana hangs the family photo on the new wall. Lui bounces on the boxes.]

226
00:07:21,881 --> 00:07:25,121
NARRATOR: We moved into a house on the other side of town.

227
00:07:25,391 --> 00:07:27,726
[Reverse: narrator answers.]

228
00:07:25,491 --> 00:07:27,606
NARRATOR: I hung our photo up first.

229
00:07:27,876 --> 00:07:31,026
[A new garden. Lui feeds two new goldfish in a new pond.]

230
00:07:27,976 --> 00:07:31,866
NARRATOR: Lui got two new goldfish. He named them Gas and Bump.

231
00:07:32,136 --> 00:07:35,286
[A creek again; this time Jaz holds the notebook.]

232
00:07:32,236 --> 00:07:39,501
NARRATOR: Now schools all over the city test their creeks every week, and send the numbers to the volcano scientists.

233
00:07:39,771 --> 00:07:42,921
[A year later: the new hill, grass on its slopes, a path to the top. The Tupou family at the rim with a kite; the city all around.]

234
00:07:39,871 --> 00:07:44,136
NARRATOR: A year later, the new hill had grass on it, and a path to the top.

235
00:07:44,406 --> 00:07:47,556
[A carved wooden sign at the foot of the path, the hill green above it.]

236
00:07:44,506 --> 00:07:48,171
NARRATOR: Mana whenua, the people of this land, gave it its name.

237
00:07:48,441 --> 00:07:51,476
[Ana at the rim, the notebook open: an old map with her circle, now with the hill drawn in its centre.]

238
00:07:48,541 --> 00:07:51,356
NARRATOR: Auckland has fifty-four volcanoes now.

239
00:07:51,626 --> 00:07:54,776
[Continues.]

240
00:07:51,726 --> 00:07:54,741
NARRATOR: The newest one is where my bedroom used to be.

241
00:07:56,011 --> 00:07:59,161
[The hill at sunset, the city lights coming on around it.]

242
00:07:56,111 --> 00:08:00,301
NARRATOR: I study volcanoes now, like Mum. I still keep a notebook.

243
00:08:01,371 --> 00:08:04,521
[End screen.]

244
00:08:01,471 --> 00:08:06,211
NARRATOR: If you had twenty minutes to leave, what would you take? Tell us in the comments.
